    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Strangely named places are found all over the world. Here are some cases in the United States and the stories behind them."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h1",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Santa Claus, Indiana"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "In 1854, a group of people settled in southwest Indiana and set up a town called Santa Fe. But when they applied to get a post office two years later, they were turned down. There was already another Santa Fe, Indiana, with a post office. The new Santa Fe would need a new name in order to be given a post office."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The townspeople held several meetings over the next few months to choose a new name, but could not agree on one. The last town meeting of the year was held on Christmas Eve. During the debate, the wind blew open the church doors, and everyone heard the ringing of "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sleigh",
            "zh": "雪橇"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " bells. The children got excited and shouted, “Santa Claus!” A light bulb went off in people’s heads, and by Christmas morning, the town had chosen its new name."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h2",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Eighty Eight, Kentucky"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Dabnie Nunnally, the town’s first postmaster, came up with the name. Nunnally didn’t think highly of his handwriting. He thought that using a number as the town’s name would make "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "illegibility",
            "zh": "字迹模糊"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " on mail less of an issue. To decide on the number, he reached into his pocket and counted his change. He had 88 cents."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "h3",
        "type": "heading",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Ding Dong, Texas"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Zulis Bell, a local businessman, ran a big store and hired C. C. Hoover, a painter, to make a sign for his business. Hoover drew two bells on the sign and wrote “Ding Dong” under the bells. As a community grew up around the store, the words "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "stuck",
            "zh": "被证明成立"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " as the name for the town."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
